Gateway reads require current active parent authority
====================================================

Gateway row lookup, list rows/counts and summary/vendor totals use the same
current database predicates: the gateway belongs to the requested organization
and is not deleted; its organization is active and not deleted; and any assigned
site belongs to that organization and is active and not deleted. Existing site
restrictions are included in the SQL predicate, before a cached response or
adapter is used. The selected gateway is refreshed from the database so an ORM
identity-map copy cannot supply an obsolete site or connection value.

An inaccessible individual gateway returns the existing opaque HTTP 404. Lists
and summary totals omit inaccessible gateways. Unassigned gateways remain
available to callers with organization-wide access. Existing role checks and
FreeSDN's hybrid site-grant policy are unchanged: a non-admin user with zero
explicit grants is still organization-wide within their role; one or more
grants restrict that user to those sites. This change must not be described as
introducing a deny-all meaning for zero grants or closing caller-grant revocation.

The correction also applies to existing live/sync/write paths that resolve their
initial gateway through _get_gw. It does not independently recheck caller roles,
sessions, API keys or site-grant records. It adds no lock across device I/O and
cannot prevent a parent from changing after admission, recall credentials already
sent, invalidate all distributed caches or undo a device operation. Saved
connection-test post-I/O guards retain their separate documented behavior.
Creation and reassignment target validation and background code that bypasses
this service require separate review. No backup storage/restore is added.

Qualification uses real PostgreSQL and HTTP routes with synthetic caller identity
and vendor replies. Eight read surfaces cover deleted/inactive organizations,
deleted/inactive/foreign sites, assigned and unassigned gateway controls, empty
explicit-grant compatibility, and stale ORM site-assignment cache reads. Separate
TCP checks commit parent mutations through an independent database session and
use fresh request sessions. They are not real hardware, TLS, login or live
caller-revocation acceptance. Full shipping results are recorded separately.
